    "resolution_orientation": {
      "what": "The axis written SECOND is the scanline length (scanlen/numscans, radiance.c:250-251), so `+X 6 +Y 1` is a 1x6 image. The -/+ direction flags are parsed and then ignored: libvips 'will not rotate/flip as the FORMAT string asks' (radiance.c:70).",
      "lines": {
        "-Y 1 +X 6": {
          "summary": "fixtures/orient_-Y_1_+X_6.hdr: 6x1, rad, radload"
        },
        "+X 6 +Y 1": {
          "summary": "fixtures/orient_+X_6_+Y_1.hdr: 1x6, rad, radload"
        },
        "-Y 1 -X 6": {
          "summary": "fixtures/orient_-Y_1_-X_6.hdr: 6x1, rad, radload"
        }
      }
    },
    "involution": {
      "what": "rad2float after float2rad after rad2float reproduces the float payload byte for byte. The sharper statement, verified over 298,240 combinations in src/radiance.rs, is that float2rad after rad2float is the IDENTITY on an RGBE quadruple whose largest mantissa is at least 128 and whose exponent byte is in 23..=255 -- exactly the normalised form any encoder emits. Below exponent 23 the 1e-32 floor collapses the pixel to all-zero, and a largest mantissa under 128 is rescaled: (127,63,31,129) becomes (254,126,62,128).",
